# Copy to `.env`. Everything here is optional: with none of it set,
# `pnpm build` produces an unsigned installer.
#
# `.env` is gitignored, and so is everything in `cert/` bar the samples. Keep it
# that way — a committed certificate, key or password lets anyone ship software
# as you.
#
# Comments must sit on their own line: Node reads an unquoted value to the end
# of the line, so a trailing comment would become part of the value.

# --- Option 1: certificate already in the Windows certificate store ----------
# Its SHA1 thumbprint, from certmgr (spaces and colons are fine).
# CERTIFICATE_THUMBPRINT=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# --- Option 2: certificate as a .pfx file ------------------------------------
# The password protecting the file. Setting this selects .pfx signing, unless
# CERTIFICATE_THUMBPRINT is also set, which wins.
# CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD=your-certificate-password

# Where the .pfx lives, relative to the repository root.
# Defaults to cert/cendrive.pfx.
# CERTIFICATE_PATH=cert/cendrive.pfx

# --- Overrides you should not normally need ----------------------------------
# Full path to signtool.exe, if the Windows SDK is somewhere unusual.
# SIGNTOOL_PATH=C:\Program Files (x86)\Windows Kits\10\bin\10.0.26100.0\x64\signtool.exe

# RFC 3161 timestamp server. Defaults to the one in src-tauri/tauri.conf.json.
# TIMESTAMP_URL=http://timestamp.digicert.com

# --- A different key altogether: the updater ---------------------------------
# The certificate above is what Windows checks before it will run the installer.
# This key signs `latest.json`, which an installed CenDrive checks before it
# replaces itself. Neither one substitutes for the other.
#
# Generate a pair once, and keep the private half out of the repository:
#
#   pnpm tauri signer generate -w cert/updater.key
#
# The public half is printed alongside it and belongs in
# src-tauri/tauri.conf.json under plugins.updater.pubkey, where it is committed
# on purpose: a build can only be updated by whoever holds the matching private
# key.
#
# Set this and the build also writes the updater artifacts — `.sig` files and
# `latest.json`. Leave it unset and the build succeeds with installers alone,
# which is why it is safe to have no key here at all.
#
# The value is either the key's contents or an absolute path to the key file. A
# relative path is read relative to whatever directory the bundler happens to
# run in, so it is not worth the guess.
# TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY=C:\Users\you\Desktop\build\cendrive\cert\updater.key

# The password chosen when the key was generated. Uncomment it even when the key
# has no password, with nothing after the `=`: left unset entirely, the signer
# stops to ask for it, and a build waiting for an answer never finishes.
# TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD=
